Hi,

I was really stunned by the number of responses I received
this morning, a few hours I sent out the query.  Many thanks
to whose who have replied to me. 

Below is the summary of the replies and the list of experts
who gave me the advice.

Many thanks again.

Regards,
Peter Chiu
======================================================================
Question:

How to make files under a different directory tree be accessed 
by the anonymous ftp account.

Summary of replies:

It is a known feature that, for security reason, the anonymous
ftp account does an implicite chroot at its start up, making only
one file system it can see, and nothing else.  This file system is 
the one where its default login directory resides (as specified in passwd).

To get round that, I have received three suggestions:

a. physically move/copy the data that you want to be visiable 
   by this account to a subdirectory under the account,

b. if the data directory concerned resides in the same file system,
   use hardlink to point to the data directory, 

c. mount the data directory using nfs with a mount point under the
   ftp directory, eg. mount local:/data /utility/ftp/pub/data.

In my particular case, the data directory tree that I want to 
be seen by the ftp account is the one NFS-mounted from another 
host running a different operating system.  So, I go for the
first suggestion and mount the data tree again onto a ftp subdirectory.

I did try the 3rd suggestion, but somehow I can only access the 
first level of subdirectory (eg /utility/ftp/pub/data/ukmodata) 
and not below (/utility/ftp/pub/data/ukmodata/*).

